// [QL] A test map for R20 / R21 / R22 - surface detail work.
//
// Hand-authored, ours, and committable: Quake Live's own map sources were never
// released, so there is no legitimate way to recompile one of its maps, and a
// recompiled .bsp would be redistributing its content anyway. This is a few
// brushes we wrote, which is neither.
//
// WHAT IT IS FOR, and it is not what it was first asked for. The question was
// whether a map with light entities could be produced to test runtime
// relighting. It can - compile with -keeplights and /maplights will report them
// - but that path is no longer needed: the lightgrid already carries a light
// direction per cell, and what has to vary across a rock is the normal, not the
// light. See R20 in TRACKER.md.
//
// What it is genuinely good for is a controlled scene: known geometry, known
// lighting, no dependency on pak00, and small enough to recompile in seconds
// while iterating on shading. And one specific experiment worth running before
// R20 Stage B is built, because Stage B is designed around its outcome:
//
//   compile twice, once with the rock shader carrying q3map_shadeAngle 60 and
//   once without, and compare the lightmaps.
//
// Stage B assumes the faceting on Quake Live's rocks is baked into the lightmap
// by q3map2's per-face lighting, and is designed to stop the lightmap being
// what shapes the surface because of it. If the two compiles look identical
// that assumption is wrong and Stage B needs rethinking before it is written,
// not after.
//
// COMPILING (q3map2, from NetRadiant-custom or GtkRadiant):
//
//   q3map2 -fs_basepath <ql> -meta qltest_light.map
//   q3map2 -fs_basepath <ql> -vis qltest_light.bsp
//   q3map2 -fs_basepath <ql> -light -fast -keeplights qltest_light.bsp
//
// Add -deluxe to the light phase to produce a BSP that /maplights reports as
// having deluxemaps - useful only for testing that detection, since none of
// Quake Live's own maps carry them.
//
// NOT COMPILED OR LOADED. Written in an environment with no q3map2 available -
// no package, and the upstream releases unreachable - so the geometry is kept
// to axis-aligned boxes, the one form that cannot be got subtly wrong. A
// faceted rock with sloped faces is what the shading work actually wants, and
// it should be added in Radiant where it can be seen; the stacked boxes below
// give hard edges to look at in the meantime.
//
// Texture names are from docs/pak-manifest.txt, not from memory.
// RE_RegisterShader returns 0 for a name the pak does not contain and reports
// nothing, so a guessed name renders nothing and says nothing about it.

